I have to agree here.  While there are many good arguments for why adventurers
always drown, dogs and cats would *never* drown from stumbling into a pool of
water unless someone held their heads under water.  Come on, now, while there
are lots of dogs and cats that don't *like* water, how many have you seen
that couldn't swim, and swim well, at that?  Answer: none.  These animals
are competant swimmers all, and the idea that they would stumble into a pool
of water (right - they hate water, unless they are a retriever - which would
only butress my argument) and then drown, is ludicrous.


Here are some questions:

	o  Should a wand or scroll of fire (or the breath of a red dragon,
	    etc.) evaporate a pool of water?  Requiring how many turns?

	o  Would an evaporated pool leave a pit behind?  Should the pit
	    contain the remains of drowned creatures or beached aquatic
	    species?

	o  Could a pool of water when jumped into mitigate (say give back
	    50%) hit points lost to a fire attack on the previous turn?

